Subject: [PATCH] device: preserve power requests across asynchronous transitions

An rfkill-driven technology can acknowledge power-off before its device has
finished removing the supplicant interface. An immediate power-on then unblocks
rfkill but loses the device enable request to PENDING_DISABLE/EBUSY, leaving a
powered technology with no interface.

Track the latest power intent independently of the active operation and
reconcile it from an idle source after completion. Cancel owned sources on
driver removal and destruction, clear pending state on immediate errors, and
avoid adding a timeout after inline completion. A timeout does not cause an
unbounded retry; a late completion still observes the latest intent.

Validated with a mocked asynchronous driver, including cancellation, errors,
late completion, removal and 200 alternating-request sequences. Hoki passed
20 rapid Wi-Fi sequences, final-off behavior and a rapid WPA2 toggle, plus
reconnect, service restart and reboot checks.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/src/device.c
+++ b/src/device.c
@@ -51,6 +51,8 @@
 							 * enable/disable
 							 * request
 							 */
+	enum connman_pending_type powered_requested;
+	guint power_reconcile;
